{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792271537,"nonce":"veFbZ6h0Y7GRR4qyFmEXhM+cJKc=","title":"Pool: every allocation and release pays a thread-local lookup on Apple targets","message":"The pool's lists are one `thread_local!`. On Apple targets each access is a call through the TLV descriptor (`_tlv_get_addr`), once per `zyntax_alloc` and once per `zyntax_free`. In chaos's loop (Python, Cranelift tiered, after 9a78d22b) that call was about 10% of samples, beside `zyntax_alloc` itself at 17% and `zyntax_free` at 10%.\n\nThe first thread to allocate now owns a process-wide share of the lists. It recognizes itself by its thread data pointer, read from `tpidrro_el0` (low three bits masked, which carry the CPU number) and compared with an atomic owner word. Every other thread keeps its thread-local share. A thread that ends leaves the share claimed; a later thread given the same pointer takes it over, and never two at once, since live threads have distinct pointers.\n\nMac: chaos at 300 iterations 1.47 s -\u003e 1.41 s user. Instructions at one iteration: nqueens 0.965, raytrace 0.972, chaos at 50 iterations 6.83G -\u003e 6.66G; deltablue, richards and go unchanged.\n\nInline allocation in compiled code would remove the remaining call per allocation; not done.","files":null}]}